{"file":{"content":"\n\n## 1. First priority: prove the rank-2 (\\to) rank-2 induction\n\nYou already have the depth-3 theorem:\n\n[\n\\frac{m(\\hat a+\\delta3^{L-1},b)}{m(\\hat a,b)}\n=============================================\n\n\\chi(r_0(\\hat a,\\delta))\\omega^{\\delta(-1)^b},\n\\qquad\nr_0=1+\\delta3^L(3\\hat a+1)^{-1}.\n]\n\nThe decisive observation is:\n\n[\n(-1)^b=(-1)^\\epsilon\n]\n\ninside the parity-split branch class\n\n[\nb\\equiv\\epsilon\\pmod2.\n]\n\nSo the parity twist becomes internal to a fixed rank-(2) fiber. The next theorem should say this structure is stable under every further descent.\n\nI would formulate the induction theorem as follows.\n\n[\n\\textbf{Theorem: Rank-2 saturation under descent.}\n]\n\nAt descent level (L), the descended primitive operator acts on functions\n\n[\nG=(G_0,G_1)\n]\n\non\n\n[\nU_{3^L}\\times \\mathbb Z/2\n]\n\nby\n\n[\n(\\mathcal N_L G)_\\epsilon(a,\\tau)\n=================================\n\n\\sum_{b\\equiv\\epsilon!!!\\pmod2}\nw_b,M_L(a,\\tau,b)\n\\left[\n\\Omega_L(a,\\tau,b)G_0(S_ba,\\tau+\\epsilon)\n+\n\\Omega_L(a,\\tau,b)^{-1}G_1(S_ba,\\tau+\\epsilon)\n\\right],\n]\n\nwhere:\n\n[\nM_L(a,\\tau,b)\\in S^1\n]\n\nis the accumulated edge multiplier, and\n\n[\n\\Omega_L(a,\\tau,b)\n]\n\nis a deep carry phase belonging to\n\n[\n\\chi(1+3^L\\mathbb Z_3).\n]\n\nThen descending from (L) to (L-1) produces another operator of the same form, with updated multipliers\n\n[\nM_{L-1},\\qquad \\Omega_{L-1},\n]\n\nand no enlargement of the internal fiber beyond the two parity components.\n\nThe proof should be purely formal once the carry ratio is written as\n\n[\n\\omega_L^{\\delta(-1)^\\epsilon}\n]\n\ninside a fixed parity class. The only possible failure mode is if the (r_0)-factors introduce dependence on the new top digit that is not absorbed into the updated (M_{L-1}). But your formula says they live in\n\n[\n1+3^L\\mathbb Z,\n]\n\nso they are exactly the next-level carry multiplier. That is the induction invariant.\n\n## 2. State the induction invariant explicitly\n\nThe paper will become much clearer if you isolate the exact invariant.\n\nAt level (L), for a lift\n\n[\n\\hat a+\\delta 3^{L-1}\n]\n\nof a residue (a\\in U_{3^{L-1}}), require that the multiplier ratio has the form\n\n[\n\\frac{M_L(\\hat a+\\delta3^{L-1},\\tau,b)}\n{M_L(\\hat a,\\tau,b)}\n====================\n\nR_L(\\hat a,\\tau,\\delta),\n\\Theta_L(\\tau,\\delta)^{(-1)^b},\n]\n\nwhere\n\n[\nR_L(\\hat a,\\tau,\\delta)\\in \\chi(1+3^L\\mathbb Z_3)\n]\n\nand\n\n[\n\\Theta_L(\\tau,\\delta)\\in \\chi(1+3^{c-1}\\mathbb Z_3)\n]\n\nor, more generally, another deep principal-unit phase.\n\nThen after parity splitting, because (b\\equiv\\epsilon), this is\n\n[\nR_L(\\hat a,\\tau,\\delta),\n\\Theta_L(\\tau,\\delta)^{(-1)^\\epsilon},\n]\n\nwhich is just a (2\\times2) matrix acting on the internal parity vector. Therefore the rank stays (2).\n\nThe proof target is:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{show the same ratio form is preserved under the descended operator.}\n}\n]\n\nThat is the entire Task A.\n\n## 3. Do not prove more than needed about carries\n\nYou do **not** need a complete closed form for every accumulated carry.\n\nFor the witness theorem, you need only the carry-depth corollary:\n\n[\nC_L(e)\\in1+3^L\\mathbb Z_3.\n]\n\nSince the final bounded level satisfies\n\n[\nc_0\\ge2,\n]\n\nall corrections created after level (L\\ge2) are invisible modulo (9):\n\n[\nC_L(e)\\equiv1\\pmod9.\n]\n\nThus the canonical witness computation\n\n[\nW\\equiv4\\pmod9,\\qquad W^2\\equiv7\\pmod9\n]\n\nsurvives all deeper descents.\n\nSo the induction theorem can be split:\n\n[\n\\textbf{Rank-2 closure}\n]\n\nand\n\n[\n\\textbf{Carry-depth corollary.}\n]\n\nThe first proves fixed rank. The second proves that the witness remains a generator.\n\nThis separation will keep the proof manageable.\n\n## 4. Formalize the canonical witness theorem\n\nOnce Task A is done, the following should become an actual theorem.\n\n[\n\\textbf{Canonical witness theorem.}\n]\n\nFor every primitive conductor (3^c) sector and every descent to bounded level (c_0\\ge2), the loop-pair witness\n\n[\n\\gamma_1=(x=1,\\ b\\equiv2),\n\\qquad\n\\gamma_2=(x=7,\\ b\\equiv4)\n]\n\nhas accumulated holonomy ratio\n\n[\nW_c(\\gamma_1,\\gamma_2)\n\\equiv4\\pmod9.\n]\n\nConsequently,\n\n[\nW_c(\\gamma_1,\\gamma_2)^2\\equiv7\\pmod9,\n]\n\nand therefore\n\n[\nv_3\\log W_c(\\gamma_1,\\gamma_2)^2=1.\n]\n\nHence\n\n[\n\\langle W_c^2\\rangle\n====================\n\n1+3\\mathbb Z/3^c\\mathbb Z.\n]\n\nThis proves strict non-coboundary for every primitive sector.\n\nBut preserve the warning:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{this proves strictness, not uniform gap, unless short-witness coverage is added.}\n}\n]\n\n## 5. Next computational target: fixed-(c_0), (c\\to\\infty)\n\nYour current table is encouraging but diagonal:\n\n[\nc_0\\text{ grows with }c.\n]\n\nThe decisive audit is fixed bounded level. After Task A, run:\n\n[\nc_0=2\n]\n\nfixed, then\n\n[\nc=3,4,5,\\dots,C\n]\n\nas far as exact arithmetic permits. Compute\n\n[\n\\delta_c(L)\n===========\n\n\\min_{\\chi\\ \\mathrm{primitive\\ mod}\\ 3^c}\n\\max_{W\\in\\mathcal W_c(L)}\n|\\chi(W^2)-1|.\n]\n\nStart with:\n\n[\nL=1,\\ 2,\\ 4,\\ 8.\n]\n\nThen try:\n\n[\nL=c,\\quad L=c^2.\n]\n\nThe important table is:\n\n[\n\\begin{array}{c|cccccc}\n\tc & 3&4&5&6&7&8\\\n\t\\hline\n\t\\delta_c(1)&&&&&&\\\n\t\\delta_c(2)&&&&&&\\\n\t\\delta_c(4)&&&&&&\\\n\t\\delta_c(c)&&&&&&\\\n\t\\delta_c(c^2)&&&&&&\n\\end{array}\n]\n\nInterpretation:\n\nIf\n\n[\n\\inf_c\\delta_c(1)>0\n]\n\nor\n\n[\n\\inf_c\\delta_c(2)>0,\n]\n\nthen the strong constant-gap conjecture is plausible.\n\nIf (\\delta_c(L)) decays for fixed (L) but stabilizes for (L=c^A), then the polylog gap is plausible.\n\nIf no (L=c^A) stabilizes, then the finite theorem may only give polynomial-in-(Q) gap. That would still be mathematically meaningful, but weaker than the H23a target.\n\n## 6. Compute exponent sets, not only (\\delta_c(L))\n\nFor proof, you need structure. For each witness\n\n[\nW\\in\\mathcal W_c(L),\n]\n\nwrite\n\n[\nW^2=(W_0^2)^{t(W)}\n]\n\ninside the cyclic principal-unit group generated by the canonical witness (W_0^2). Then define the exponent set\n\n[\nT_c(L)\n======\n\n{t(W)\\bmod 3^{c-1}:W\\in\\mathcal W_c(L)}.\n]\n\nThen\n\n[\n\\delta_c(L)\n===========\n\n\\min_{\\substack{k\\bmod3^{c-1}\\3\\nmid k}}\n\\max_{t\\in T_c(L)}\n\\left|\ne^{2\\pi i kt/3^{c-1}}-1\n\\right|.\n]\n\nThis is a pure finite Fourier covering problem.\n\nNow your proof target becomes:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\tT_c(L)\\text{ is not contained in a short arc under any primitive frequency.}\n}\n]\n\nFor constant gap, (T_c(L_0)) must be a very strong bounded witness set. For polylog gap, (T_c(c^A)) must spread sufficiently.\n\nThis is the exact place where “mode spreading” re-enters, but now in a finite cyclic group rather than a vague comparison graph.\n\n## 7. Look for an affine recurrence for (T_c(L))\n\nThe rank-2 descent likely induces a recurrence on witness exponents:\n\n[\nt_{j+1}= \\alpha_j t_j+\\beta_j\n\\pmod{3^{c-1}},\n]\n\nor for a set,\n\n[\nT_{j+1}\n\\supseteq\n\\alpha T_j+\\beta\n]\n\nwith several ((\\alpha,\\beta)).\n\nIf the available maps include at least two affine transformations with multipliers not both congruent to (1\\pmod3), then (T_c(L)) may expand rapidly in (3)-adic digits.\n\nThis is the next symbolic proof direction:\n\n[\n\\text{rank-2 carry recursion}\n\\Rightarrow\n\\text{affine dynamics on witness exponents}\n\\Rightarrow\n\\text{rapid coverage of primitive characters}.\n]\n\nTry to extract the maps on (t) directly from the multiplier tables.\n\n## 8. What would prove constant gap?\n\nA fixed finite witness set (\\mathcal W_c(L_0)) gives constant gap iff its exponent set (T_c(L_0)) is uniformly covering primitive frequencies.\n\nFor a fixed number of (3)-adic units, this is a strong condition. It can happen if the exponents themselves depend on (c) in a way that samples high digits, not merely as fixed (3)-adic limits.\n\nYour diagonal evidence suggests something like that may be happening. But fixed (c_0), (c\\to\\infty) is the real test.\n\nIf (T_c(L_0)) reduces to finitely many fixed (3)-adic units independent of (c), then constant gap probably fails, because a primitive character with small frequency can be near (1) on all of them.\n\nSo the audit should answer:\n\n[\n\\text{Do bounded-length witnesses move with }c\\text{ through high digits?}\n]\n\nIf yes, constant gap is plausible. If no, expect only polylog or polynomial.\n\n## 9. What to add to the manuscript now\n\nI would add a subsection titled:\n\n[\n\\textbf{The remaining quantitative problem: coverage, not generation.}\n]\n\nState:\n\n[\n\\text{Canonical generation proves exact non-coboundarity.}\n]\n\n[\n\\text{Uniform gap requires controlled-length coverage.}\n]\n\nThen define:\n\n[\nT_c(L),\\qquad \\delta_c(L).\n]\n\nMake (\\delta_c(L)) the main conjectural quantity. This will make the paper’s status perfectly clear.\n\nThen add the rank-2 theorem as:\n\n[\n\\textbf{Theorem: rank-2 parity-twisted descent at depth (3).}\n]\n\nand the remaining induction as:\n\n[\n\\textbf{Task A / Conjectural induction step.}\n]\n\nIf you already have a convincing structural proof of saturation, call it a proposition with proof sketch; otherwise keep it as “remaining mechanical induction.”\n\n## 10. What to do after Task A\n\nAfter Task A and the fixed-(c_0) audit, there are three possible outcomes.\n\n### Outcome 1: fixed (L_0) coverage survives\n\nThen prove constant gap by finite witness compactness.\n\nTarget theorem:\n\n[\n\\exists L_0,\\delta_0>0\n\\quad\n\\forall c,\\quad\n\\delta_c(L_0)\\ge\\delta_0.\n]\n\nThis gives:\n\n[\n\\inf_Q\\kappa_Q>0.\n]\n\n### Outcome 2: only (L=O(c^A)) coverage survives\n\nThen prove polylog gap.\n\nTarget theorem:\n\n[\n\\exists A,\\delta_0>0\n\\quad\n\\forall c,\\quad\n\\delta_c(c^A)\\ge\\delta_0.\n]\n\nThis gives:\n\n[\n\\kappa_Q\\gg(\\log Q)^{-2A}.\n]\n\n### Outcome 3: coverage requires (L\\asymp 3^c)\n\nThen the finite operator theorem yields only polynomial-in-(Q) gap, and the H23a quantitative target must be weakened or supplemented by another mechanism.\n\nThis would not invalidate the finite-shadow object, but it would change its usefulness for the global Collatz program.\n\n## Bottom line\n\nYou are now very close to finishing the **qualitative finite operator theorem**:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{exact descent + rank-2 carry recursion + canonical witness}\n\t\\Rightarrow\n\t\\text{strict finite-level non-coboundary gap.}\n}\n]\n\nThe remaining quantitative theorem is not generation. It is:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{controlled-length witness coverage.}\n}\n]\n\nSo the next targets are:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{Task A: rank-2-to-rank-2 induction;}\n}\n]\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{fixed-}c_0\\text{ computation of }\\delta_c(L);\n}\n]\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{exponent-set dynamics }T_c(L)\\text{ and rapid coverage.}\n}\n]\n\nThat is the cleanest route forward.\n","filePath":"/home/john/mynotes/deterministic_equidistribution_theory/critiques/dc_11.md","numLines":564,"startLine":1,"totalLines":564},"type":"text"}